fication  exceeds 90 percent these specialized  models must still be robust to  classification errors  as you can see we can get significant  word error rate improvement  over just a generalized risk order  the general rescorer also just uses the  lattice  in order to re-rank  however adding attention to audio  provides additional context for  discriminating between alternate  hypotheses  leading to a 7.7 percent improvement  in word error rate  these language models are also hard to  adapt  personalization of entities like contact  names or playlists are essential to  correct recognition  new catalog entries for songs and movies  are equally challenging  these also have to scale across millions  of users  and must support very frequent updates  the answer lies in composite models  the set of entities that a user may  reference is constantly growing  or are dependent on the user and the  application  for example for a communications  application we need to pull names from  the list of contacts  for many other scenarios we need global  catalogues  for example geographical locations  or an app may provide the entity list  for its own purposes such as the  characters of a tv show  or rooms in a home we want the lm  the language model to access these lists  of entity names  but how do we know when to invoke them  the standard solution is to build  language models in terms of class labels  such as location the language model  predicts the class  then we have a separate model to predict  the possible class members  given the type of class the membership  models  can be context dependent or personalized  however to train class-based models we  need those classes or entity types  annotated in our data annotating  significant amounts of data is very  expensive  and adding new classes would require  re-labeling all data  and in the case of third-party  applications where each application can  have its own entity types it's not very  clear  how to scale the labeling process  also note that in this example the  location in the sentence is  my hometown while the entity model only  contains hometown  the annotation process itself can  introduce discrepancies  can we do without these animations it  turns out that we can  the basic idea is to let a neural model  learn the context  under which different classes become  good predictors of  words we have a set of component models  that can generate  words but some generate only specific  entities  let's take a communications interaction  as an example  alexa called shazad mevala  we want to leverage the user's  personalized contact list  to guide and personalize the second pass  url and rescoring  we combine the components predictions  using an attention mechanism  but we also have to learn which models  are good at generating particular  portions of an input query  for example a personalized contact list  slot  is not really good at generating words  from the carrier phrase in our example  alexa call but are good at generating  slot words  shazad nivawala  the model learns which component model  should be activated  at particular positions of a query and  in addition which models are good at  predicting  next word and position based on the  context  we use a context encoder to make a fixed  size  representation from the sequence of  previous words  this is the query in the attention  mechanism  but also the input to guide the  activation component  to help with the learning as to which  model should be activated at any  position  this is matched against the learnable  embeddings  that represent each component model  these serve as the key  in the attention mechanism  the attention mechanism computes the  weighting of the different  component predictions according to the  match  between the queries and the keys  so far this is just context dependent  interpolation of models  but some components can generate only  parts of a sentence  they need to be told when to start  generating their content  we use a novel mechanism we call  activation  which resets the state of a component to  tell it to start generating its content  activation is also computed by matching  context queries against component keys  but each component decides independently  and probably probabilistically when to  activate  for training the activation model we use  reinforcement learning  there is no need for label data because  bad propagating the errors allows the  model to learn  when to call on each component based on  context  and what the component predicts  we applied the neural composition model  to combine a default language model with  personalized contact lists for  communication tasks  on in-domain utterances perplexity was  cut in half  and recognition improved by 10 relative  no degradation was found on out of  domain data so the model  learned not to pay attention to the  contact name component in those cases  even without training unlabeled data  for far field applications interactions  are usually explicitly triggered via  invoking the wake word  but termination must be computed by  automatically  inferring when a user is done speaking  end to early and you may cut off the  speaker  wait too long and you add latency or  risk  introducing unintended insertions the  system must learn to respond  instantly at the end of a spoken query  but also be patient if the user  hesitates  or pauses during speech  end pointing uses no explicit feature  extraction  we instead fuse multiple signals  including  acoustic embeddings for example silence  one best hypothesis from the asr decoder  as well as expected pauses based on the  linguistic position  within the sentence  we also want alexa to effectively use  features  beyond just speech recognition in order  to enhance the  overall customer experience whisper  is a great example when you whisper to  alexa  she will whisper back that is a magical  experience for customers  for example when you set alarms late in  the evening  while your family is sleeping or when  you're checking calendar weather or  traffic  early each morning while others are  still asleep  the whisper feature allows alexa to  understand that this is a quiet  environment  and behave like humans would do  whisper speech is mainly characterized  as unvoiced speech  studies of spectrograms have suggested  that whisper speech  overall has less energy at lower  frequency bands compared to normal  speech  we came up with a system that consists  of an lstm neural network  trained and evaluated on recordings of  human interactions  with voice controlled far-field devices  in whisper  and normal modes but it's not just about  the science on the detection side that  makes this feature magical  we were also able to create a  comfortable whispering voice  from text to speech and response the  response  in kind is what makes the experience  magical  if you allow alexa to recognize your  voice  she can greet you by name and  personalize your experience for example  music playlists  shopping carts etc speaker enrollment  commences with the user speaking four  short phrases  we designed these phrases not just to be  simple and topical  but also to provide the greatest  phonetic coverage  allowing us to deliver the best  recognition accuracy  a lexus speaker id system uses a  combination of two different model types  the text dependent model knows what  you're saying ahead of time  with alexa most utterances start with  the wake word  so it matches the way you said alexa now  with the way you said alexa before then  there's a text  independent model which matches your  voice regardless of what you're saying  moving both these systems to full neural  and combining them  has given us an order of magnitude  improvement in speaker id accuracy  instead of fusing the decision scores of  multiple models  for example a straight average we fuse  the embeddings  generated by the component models which  are all neural  this allows us to let the model learn to  estimate  any missing embeddings from the ones  that are present  for example if the utterance did not  start with the wake word  this neural fusion method gave up to a  15  relative reduction in overall false  reject rate  and when one of the inputs is missing  the false reject reduction  was up to 53  now let's switch topics and talk a  little bit about edge processing or  processing on the device itself  when you're processing on the cloud you  have an unlimited amount  of computation power to run the powerful  machine learning algorithms that are  required  for voice assistance now imagine you  want to fit  this functionality and squeeze it into  a device no bigger than a tin can  you have almost no memory megabytes  instead of gigabytes  a fraction of the compute and no  dedicated  system resources  how does one accomplish something like  that  in the latest amazon devices we've  incorporated our new az-1 neural edge  processor  which is a neural network accelerator  that allows us  to run these sophisticated ml algorithms  efficiently and quickly right on the  device  this neural processor will allow us to  move all audio processing  to the device current echo devices  listen for the wake word on the device  and once detected they stream  audio to the cloud for all for the  processing  these new devices give us the capability  to execute  asr speaker id and whisper on the device  itself  such that only a textual representation  needs to be sent to the cloud  this means faster response times  especially  on narrowband with connections  let's now review for a minute how  classic hybrid asr architectures work  a hybrid esr system is comprised of  multiple components  a signal processing front end takes raw  audio frames as input  and produces a stream of feature vectors  that serve as inputs  to the acoustic model the acoustic model  then produces phoneme probabilities  for each given frame the decoder then  combines  the phoneme predictions with the  pronunciation lexicon  as well as the engram-based language  model  to execute a large search over all  possible combinations of words  to find the most likely sequence  these multiple components when packaged  together especially when inclusive  of the large language model results in  an extremely large  overall asr model size  these components are replaced when  moving to an end-to-end architecture  the technology we adopted and extended  for all neural asr  is commonly referred to as rnn  transducer  or rnnt it consists of an encoder  network that transforms the audio  signals  into a new frame by frame representation  over time  a prediction network that encodes the  respectively current  word sequence and a network that brings  both pieces of  information together to yield  probabilities for all potential next  words  or word pieces with an end to end model  you no longer have the overhead of the  various separate components  or those giant search trees instead you  now have a full neural representation  which by itself reduces the size of the  model  to 1 100 the original size  various quantization techniques minimize  the memory and compute footprint even  further  without losing any accuracy these models  can then be deployed on a devices  and executed using our amazon euro  processor  but what about even smaller devices like  wearables  or automotive applications some of these  may not even have  internet connectivity  one of the techniques is obviously model  compression and quantization  quantization helps to reduce model size  computation  and latency the main problem is that  post training quantization  leads to severe performance degradation  to achieve the best performance on  device  we need to incorporate the hardware  architecture into the model training  itself  with this solution we were able to  reduce the model size  by 4x without degrading the performance  of a speech recognition model  furthermore this solution requires  minimal overhead for memory and  computation  which makes it well suited for models  with large number of parameters  such as speech recognition or even text  to speech  another approach to reduce footprint and  latency are signaled intent models  the conventional approach to spoken  language understanding  uses two different components to  sequentially process  a spoken entrance the asr model first  converts the customer audio to a text  transcription followed by the natural  language understanding model that  converts this  transcription to a semantic  interpretation  this approach has worked well so far  especially with the adoption of neural  technologies for both esr and nlu models  however this modular design has a few  drawbacks  the component models are trained  independently with separate objectives  can be word error rate for asr and cross  entropy for nlu the errors made by the  asr model  can lead to an incorrect nlu model  prediction  since these errors are absent from the  nlu training data  finally this design assigns equal  importance to each word  in the three examples shown here the  word error rate for each utterance is 25  however missing a key word from the  utterance will lead to a non-recoverable  nlu error the word error metric does not  differentiate this effect  when looking at the final model  performance  an approach to address this problem is  to employ models that can predict the  spoken  language understanding output directly  from speech  these end-to-end slu models are trained  on a single optimization objective  that is to correctly predict the  utterance interpretation  these models are robust to component  specific errors  moreover they are resource and latency  efficient  instead of having multiple models to get  the final interpretation  we now have a single model often with  fewer total number of parameters that  can predict the final interpretation  this finally leads to smaller sizes and  overall less computational overhead  looking a bit to the future virtual  assistants are still  early in their revolution they work  really well for command and control type  interactions  controlling smart home devices answering  questions  playing music where we really want to  get though  are true conversational assistants  alexa's natural turn-taking feature  which we plan to launch  next year will let customers interact  with alexa more naturally  without the need to repeat the wake word  the features  ai will be able to recognize when users  have finished speaking  when the speech is directed at the  device and when it is not  and whether or not a reply is expected  natural tone taking uses acoustic cues  to distinguish device directed and noise  non-device directed speech  it adds other cues such as visual  information from devices with cameras  on device algorithms processes process  images  and audio infer from the speaker's body  position where they're  likely to be addressing alexa and infer  from the audio whether it is directed to  the device  or to someone else  we still have many challenges to solve  self-correction capabilities  code switching interpreting  paralinguistic cues from customers  and true high fidelity long-form  conversational asr  are just some examples  though it has evolved over several  decades thanks to steady progress  in academic government and industry  research  speech technology has seen rapid  progress over the last few years  this is thanks mainly to deep learning  algorithms  as well as the low-cost compute hardware  necessary to support them  as we also saw in this presentation  building a virtual assistant  requires the orchestration of several  critical  sub tasks like wake word detection  endpointing asr nw tts etc  and while we have made great progress it  is still day one  and a great time to be a speech  researcher  thank you very much for your time and  attention i will now be happy to answer  any questions that you may have


